  "title": "On Screen Presence — Full Collection",
  "summary": "The computer monitor has been a viewing apparatus for art since the early 1970s. Yet, after 50 years, most galleries only recently adapted to virtual exhibition due to the pandemic, often resorting to glorified e-commerce pages called OVRs. These emphasize galleries as retailers and highlight the screen as a mediator blocking corporeal experience.\n\nWhile 3D virtual environments exist, they often fail to replace the absence of physical presence and don't account for medium specificity. The New York Times began highlighting online exhibitions, but these still presented a flattened, screen-mediated experience.\n\nThe exhibition \"On Screen Presence\" began as a response to scrolling through decontextualized artwork. It offers live-streamed presence of artists in their studios among tangible components.",
